.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt TAP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tap ,
.Nm tun
.Nd network interface whose other end is a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/net/tun
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The character device
.Pa /dev/net/tun ,
10:200 as on Linux, makes a network interface whose wire is a file
descriptor: each frame the stack sends on the interface is one
.Xr read 2
of the descriptor, and each
.Xr write 2
or
.Xr writev 2
of the descriptor is one frame the interface receives.
A TAP carries Ethernet frames; QEMU's
.Ql -netdev tap
joins a guest's network card to one, so that the host routes through the
guest or the guest through the host.
A TUN carries IP datagrams with no link header, as a VPN's daemon uses
it.
.Pp
A program opens the device and gives the descriptor an interface with
.Dv TUNSETIFF ,
whose argument is a
.Vt struct ifreq
with the name in
.Va ifr_name
and the flags in
.Va ifr_flags .
When an interface has the name, the call attaches it: a TUN for
.Dv IFF_TUN
or a TAP for
.Dv IFF_TAP ,
when the caller is its owner and in its group, as
.Dv TUNSETOWNER
and
.Dv TUNSETGROUP
set them, or has
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN ;
.Dv IFF_TUN_EXCL
refuses it.
Otherwise the call, which needs
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
makes a TUN for
.Dv IFF_TUN
or a TAP for
.Dv IFF_TAP .
.Dv IFF_NO_PI
leaves out the packet information below, and
.Dv IFF_ONE_QUEUE
is ignored, as on Linux; the interface keeps both from the call that
attaches it.
An empty name is
.Ql tun%d
for a TUN and
.Ql tap%d
for a TAP.
A name with a
.Ql %
is a template, whose one
.Ql %d
takes a digit as Linux's dev_alloc_name chooses it: a number is taken
when the name it gives exists and reads back through the template, with
.Xr sscanf 3 ,
as that number, and the template takes the first number not taken.
A digit after the
.Ql %d
is read back with the number, so with
.Li a01
present
.Ql a%d1
fails, and with
.Li a00
present
.Ql a%d0
gives
.Li a10 .
A name may not be
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql \&.. ,
or hold
.Ql / ,
.Ql \&:
or white space, as Linux's dev_valid_name judges.
The call writes the name it gave back to
.Va ifr_name ,
with the flags as the caller gave them.
It reads and writes the caller's whole
.Vt struct ifreq ,
32 bytes for an i386 program and 40 for an x86-64 one.
The interface starts down with an MTU of 1500 and has a carrier while a
descriptor holds it.
A TAP has a random locally administered station address.
A TUN has none: it is of type none, 65534, point-to-point with no ARP and
no neighbour discovery, and its IPv6 link-local address has a random
identifier, as Linux's random address mode gives it.
.Pp
For IPv4 a TUN address may name a peer and its prefix, for example
.Ql ip addr add 192.0.2.1 peer 198.51.100.2/32 dev tun0 .
The peer's prefix is routed through the TUN while the interface is up.
.Dv SIOCGIFDSTADDR
and
.Dv SIOCSIFDSTADDR
read and set the first address's peer.
.Pp
A packet socket of type
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
reports a TUN hardware address length of zero and accepts that address
on sends.
.Pp
A read returns one frame, whole, or its first bytes when the buffer is
shorter, and
.Xr readv 2
scatters one frame across its vector; a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
returns
.Er EAGAIN
when no frame waits.
A TUN's frame is the IP datagram alone.
Without
.Dv IFF_NO_PI
each frame read is behind Linux's
.Vt struct tun_pi :
a 16-bit word of flags, in the machine's order, then the frame's protocol,
an EtherType, big-endian.
The flags are
.Dv TUN_PKT_STRIP ,
1, when the buffer is shorter than the whole, and a buffer shorter than
the 4 bytes of the header is
.Er EINVAL ,
and loses the frame, as on Linux.
Each interface holds up to 1024 frames for its reader, as Linux's tun
queues 1000; a frame sent while the queue is full is dropped and counted
in the interface's transmit drops.
.Pp
A write, or a
.Xr writev 2
whose vector is gathered into one, is one frame: without
.Dv IFF_NO_PI
behind a
.Vt struct tun_pi ,
then for a TAP an Ethernet frame of 14 to 1514 bytes, and for a TUN an IP
datagram of up to 1500, received as the protocol of the header, or with
.Dv IFF_NO_PI
as IPv4 or IPv6 by its version.
A longer one is taken unread and counted as dropped, so that the writer
goes on.
A TUN's datagram of no IP version without the header is
.Er EINVAL
and counted as dropped, as on Linux.
A
.Xr writev 2
of no bytes returns 0 and sends nothing, as Linux's.
.Xr poll 2
reports the descriptor readable while a frame waits and always
writable.
.Pp
When the last descriptor of the open closes, the interface loses its
carrier and its queued frames and, unless
.Dv TUNSETPERSIST
keeps it, is removed with its addresses, routes and neighbours.
.Ss Requests
.Bl -tag -width TUNGETVNETHDRSZ
.It Dv TUNGETFEATURES
Writes
.Dv IFF_TUN | IFF_TAP | IFF_NO_PI | IFF_ONE_QUEUE
to the
.Vt unsigned int
at the argument, the flags
.Dv TUNSETIFF
takes.
.It Dv TUNSETIFF
As above.
.It Dv TUNGETIFF
Writes the interface's name and flags:
.Dv IFF_TUN
or
.Dv IFF_TAP ,
those the attach kept and
.Dv IFF_PERSIST
when set.
.It Dv TUNSETPERSIST
A nonzero argument keeps the interface after its descriptor closes.
.It Dv TUNSETOWNER , TUNSETGROUP
The argument, a user or group of the caller's user namespace, becomes the
interface's owner or group, which may attach it without
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.It Dv TUNSETOFFLOAD
Takes 0, no offload; any other flag is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv TUNGETSNDBUF
Writes
.Dv INT_MAX ,
Linux's default.
.It Dv TUNSETSNDBUF , TUNSETLINK , TUNSETNOCSUM , TUNSETDEBUG
Taken and ignored.
.It Dv TUNSETVNETHDRSZ , TUNGETVNETHDRSZ , TUNSETVNETLE , TUNGETVNETLE , TUNSETVNETBE , TUNGETVNETBE , TUNSETQUEUE
.Er EINVAL :
there is no virtio header and one queue.
.El
.Sh LIMITS
The interfaces take the slots of the four the kernel has besides
.Li lo .
No virtio header and so no offload, and one queue.
Interfaces are made only in the first network namespace.
A frame written is at most 1514 bytes, a TUN's datagram 1500, where Linux
takes up to 64 KiB.
A name is at most 7 bytes, where Linux takes 15, and a template's
.Ql %d
takes the digits 0 to 9 alone.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/net/tun
.It Pa /dev/net/tun
The device.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give a QEMU guest the interface
.Li tap0
and route to it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# qemu-system-x86_64 -netdev tap,id=n0,ifname=tap0,script=no,downscript=no \e
    -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0 ...
# ifconfig tap0 192.168.100.1 netmask 255.255.255.0 up
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBADFD
A read, write or request that needs the interface came before
.Dv TUNSETIFF ,
or the descriptor's interface is gone.
.It Er EBUSY
.Dv TUNSETIFF
named an interface another descriptor holds, or one that exists under
.Dv IFF_TUN_EXCL .
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv TUNSETIFF
on a descriptor that has an interface.
.It Er EFAULT
The
.Vt struct ifreq ,
or a buffer of a read or write, is not the caller's.
When only the copy of the name back to the caller faults, the
descriptor keeps the interface
.Dv TUNSETIFF
made or took, as on Linux.
.It Er EINVAL
.Dv TUNSETIFF
with neither
.Dv IFF_TUN
nor
.Dv IFF_TAP ,
with
.Dv IFF_VNET_HDR
or
.Dv IFF_MULTI_QUEUE ,
with a name too long or one a name may not be, a template with a
.Ql %
that is not one
.Ql %d ,
or the name of an interface of another kind;
.Dv TUNSETOWNER
or
.Dv TUNSETGROUP
with an id the caller's namespace does not map;
a read shorter than the packet information; a write shorter than the
packet information or a TAP's Ethernet header, or a TUN's datagram of no
IP version.
.It Er ENFILE
Every digit of the template's
.Ql %d
is taken, or the first not taken gives a name that exists.
.It Er ENOSPC
Every interface of the kernel's is taken.
.It Er ENOTTY
An unknown request.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Dv TUNSETIFF
in a network namespace other than the first.
.It Er EPERM
.Dv TUNSETIFF
without
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN
to make an interface, or to attach one whose owner or group the caller
is not.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ifconfig 1 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr vhost-net 4 ,
.Xr virtio-net 4
.Sh HISTORY
The device follows Linux's drivers/net/tun.c and the numbers of
linux/if_tun.h.
